Z||000001||RossT||09-26-2000||01:44 AM||||Actually I just sold my 792 mile 88gt!!  There is a market out there for collectable fieros.  It did take me 3 months to sell it, but it did sell.  What I found is that there are alot of low mileage cars out there for sale, but most people are asking a ton of money, like 15-$18k.  For the most part those cars are not selling. Those cars that are listed from 10-$13K are selling, just not very fast. The other thing that I found is that most "buyers" are looking for loaded cars with leather and performance sound system (stock sub).  If your low mileage car does not have these.... good luck.<P>I went the whole summer without an offer on my car, then during the first week of September I had 2 offers and one guy wanting to send me a deposit.  go figure that..<P>even though I did sell my 792 mile car, I still have my 1,100 mile 88gt t-tops.  And NO, I will not be selling this one (but then, thats what I said about the other one).<P>Later<P>[This message has been edited by RossT (edited 09-26-2000).]<p>[This message has been edited by RossT (edited 09-26-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Phil||09-26-2000||09:04 AM||||Personally I wouldn't want a low milage Fiero, you can't drive the blasted thing. I've put on 36000 mi since Aug '99.||||reg||1
Z||000003||RossT||09-27-2000||12:07 AM||||Actually the guy that bought my car was going to use it as a daily driver. He looked at what 13K would get him on a new car.   Maybee a geo metro... And concluded that he would rather have a "new" fiero.  He does not intend to make money on it, only have fun driving it.<P>But your point is well taken..<P>Ross||||reg||1
